WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.257
It would make my imaginary
marking pen sigh.

00:00:00.913 --> 00:00:03.783
Now imagine some walls
between air sacs break down.

00:00:04.108 --> 00:00:05.816
The remaining spaces become bigger.

00:00:06.458 --> 00:00:07.608
Does bigger mean better?

00:00:08.342 --> 00:00:12.920
It sounds better, but fewer larger
spaces can have less total surface area.

00:00:13.432 --> 00:00:15.462
Gas exchange becomes less efficient.

00:00:16.221 --> 00:00:16.992
Exactly.

00:00:17.319 --> 00:00:18.893
That helps explain emphysema.

00:00:19.363 --> 00:00:22.810
The size of a space is not the
same as its total exchange surface.

00:00:23.232 --> 00:00:26.221
The folded paper prop shows
how much surface can fit into a

00:00:26.221 --> 00:00:30.697
small volume, but actual alveoli
are air sacs, not paper folds.

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:33.108
Structure linked to function.

00:00:33.563 --> 00:00:35.733
The same exam skill keeps returning

00:00:36.086 --> 00:00:39.057
Is exhaled air pure carbon dioxide?

00:00:39.816 --> 00:00:40.834
I know this trick.

00:00:41.143 --> 00:00:42.377
There is still oxygen in

00:00:42.377 --> 00:00:46.394
it, otherwise my answer would
deserve the dramatic teacher tissues.

00:00:47.197 --> 00:00:47.776
Correct.

00:00:48.298 --> 00:00:52.360
Typical classroom values are about
twenty-one percent oxygen in inhaled

00:00:52.387 --> 00:00:55.203
air and sixteen percent in exhaled air.

00:00:55.918 --> 00:00:59.155
Carbon dioxide rises from
roughly zero point zero four

00:00:59.157 --> 00:01:01.059
percent to about four percent.

00:01:01.727 --> 00:01:01.973
So

00:01:02.033 --> 00:01:02.578
exhaled

00:01:02.594 --> 00:01:07.381
air has less oxygen and more
carbon dioxide, not no oxygen

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:08.993
and all carbon dioxide.

00:01:09.722 --> 00:01:13.331
Nitrogen is still the biggest
component, roughly seventy-eight

00:01:13.335 --> 00:01:15.632
percent, and changes very little.

00:01:16.017 --> 00:01:17.591
These are approximate figures.

00:01:17.896 --> 00:01:21.572
Breathing pattern, activity,
and how samples are collected

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:23.516
affect the exact composition.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:26.371
What about fogging up a cold mirror?

00:01:27.355 --> 00:01:31.935
Exhaled air is usually warmer and contains
more water vapor than the air you inhaled.

00:01:32.386 --> 00:01:36.293
On a cold surface, some water
condenses into tiny droplets.

00:01:36.653 --> 00:01:38.595
Water vapor itself is invisible.

00:01:39.371 --> 00:01:43.154
Can we test the carbon dioxide
instead of just believing the numbers?

00:01:43.895 --> 00:01:47.919
In a supervised lab, limewater
turns cloudy or milky when

00:01:47.922 --> 00:01:49.853
carbon dioxide passes through it.

00:01:50.257 --> 00:01:54.050
Compare equal volumes of exhaled
air and room air with the same

00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:55.647
starting volume of limewater.

00:01:56.234 --> 00:01:58.769
Exhaled air usually
makes it cloudy faster.

00:01:59.535 --> 00:02:04.410
Same amount of air, same limewater,
same conditions, otherwise I could

00:02:04.412 --> 00:02:06.851
win by bubbling ten times as much.

00:02:07.615 --> 00:02:08.404
Exactly.

00:02:08.755 --> 00:02:12.764
Use the teacher's approved apparatus,
eye protection, and instructions.

00:02:13.055 --> 00:02:15.761
Never suck limewater
through a straw or taste it.

00:02:16.103 --> 00:02:19.231
Our video uses an animation,
not a home experiment.

00:02:20.043 --> 00:02:22.401
Positive test, clear limewater

00:02:22.433 --> 00:02:23.511
becomes cloudy.

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:25.624
Not cloudy becomes clear.

00:02:26.272 --> 00:02:26.642
Good.

00:02:27.086 --> 00:02:28.660
Explain the result carefully.

00:02:28.983 --> 00:02:33.163
The controlled comparison supports
more carbon dioxide in exhaled air.

00:02:33.627 --> 00:02:37.101
It does not tell you an exact
percentage by looking at cloudiness.

00:02:37.486 --> 00:02:39.177
You have just jogged gently.

00:02:39.578 --> 00:02:41.802
Why do breathing rate and depth increase?

00:02:42.633 --> 00:02:46.059
My muscle cells respire
faster to release more energy.

00:02:46.552 --> 00:02:49.629
They need oxygen delivered
and carbon dioxide removed.

00:02:50.382 --> 00:02:50.757
Good.

00:02:51.213 --> 00:02:53.274
Breathing rate is breaths per minute.

00:02:53.581 --> 00:02:56.098
Depth is the amount of
air moved per breath.

00:02:56.470 --> 00:02:59.939
Heart rate also rises,
helping transport gases.

00:03:00.317 --> 00:03:04.642
Control involves the brain, signals
from movement, and sensors responding

00:03:04.642 --> 00:03:07.642
to carbon dioxide, acidity, and oxygen.

00:03:08.418 --> 00:03:13.044
So it is not just one oxygen alarm,
and carbon dioxide does not have to

00:03:13.045 --> 00:03:14.995
build up endlessly during exercise.

00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:16.325
Correct.

00:03:16.871 --> 00:03:20.088
Increased breathing usually helps
keep the blood gases regulated.

00:03:20.751 --> 00:03:25.155
After exercise, extra oxygen supports
recovery, including processing

00:03:25.155 --> 00:03:27.233
lactate and restoring energy stores.

00:03:27.649 --> 00:03:31.464
The classroom phrase oxygen debt
is a simplification, not just

00:03:31.473 --> 00:03:33.139
vacuuming acid from muscles.

00:03:33.971 --> 00:03:37.589
Could I count breaths before and
after the same gentle activity?

00:03:38.291 --> 00:03:40.880
With your teacher's
safe activity plan, yes.

00:03:41.189 --> 00:03:44.953
Keep the activity, duration,
and counting method consistent.

00:03:45.492 --> 00:03:48.141
Repeat and compare with
your own resting value.

00:03:48.614 --> 00:03:50.143
Stop if you feel unwell.

00:03:50.521 --> 00:03:53.358
This is not a competition
or a fitness diagnosis.

00:03:54.152 --> 00:03:58.590
And protect the tiny alveoli because
their walls are doing important work.

00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:00.997
Smoking damages that system.

00:04:01.547 --> 00:04:04.795
Tar is a mixture containing
harmful substances, including

00:04:04.799 --> 00:04:07.242
carcinogens, and smoke damages cilia.

00:04:07.817 --> 00:04:10.573
Mucus and particles are
cleared less effectively.

00:04:10.964 --> 00:04:15.637
Carbon monoxide binds strongly to
hemoglobin, reducing oxygen transport.

00:04:16.419 --> 00:04:19.734
Nicotine is the addictive
substance, not the gas that

00:04:19.734 --> 00:04:21.776
steals hemoglobin's oxygen seats.

00:04:22.575 --> 00:04:23.364
Exactly.

00:04:23.706 --> 00:04:28.767
Long-term smoking increases the risks of
bronchitis, lung cancer, and emphysema.

00:04:29.356 --> 00:04:32.289
Fine dust and air pollution
can also harm lungs.

00:04:32.612 --> 00:04:34.729
We do not need a smoking demonstration.

00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:37.381
Clear diagrams explain the mechanisms.

00:04:38.165 --> 00:04:39.499
Final exam challenge.

00:04:39.854 --> 00:04:40.000
I am ready, but please hide the answers
